{"name":"Sunflower Seed","hex":"#ffe3a9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunflower seed","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A seed of a sunflower, especially roasted and eaten as a snack; also used as bird food and in the manufacture of sunflower oil."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunflower_seed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sunflower seed","blocks":[{"text":"A sunflower seed is a seed from a sunflower. There are three types of commonly used sunflower seeds: linoleic, high oleic, and sunflower oil seeds. Each variety has its own unique levels of monounsaturated, saturated, and polyunsaturated fats. The information in this article refers mainly to the linoleic variety."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Sunflower_Seeds_Kaldari.jpg/330px-Sunflower_Seeds_Kaldari.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunflower_seed","wikidata":"Q1076906"},{"title":"Common sunflower","blocks":[{"text":"The common sunflower is a large annual forb in the daisy family Asteraceae. The domesticated form of common sunflower is harvested for its edible seeds, which come in two types: oil and confectionery seeds. Oilseed sunflowers are widely grown globally and represent the fourth most used vegetable oil in the world. They also are used widely as bird food or as food for livestock. In contrast, confectionery sunflower seeds are often eaten as a snack food or in baking. There also are horticultural sunflower varieties that are used as plantings in domestic gardens for aesthetics. Wild plants are known for their multiple flower heads, whereas the domestic sunflower often possesses a single large flower head atop an unbranched stem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg/330px-Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_sunflower","wikidata":"Q171497"},{"title":"Seed","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a seed is a plant structure containing an embryo and stored nutrients in a protective coat called a testa. More generally, the term seed means anything that can be sown, which may include seed and husk or tuber. Seeds are the product of the ripened ovule, after the embryo sac is fertilized by sperm from pollen, forming a zygote. The embryo within a seed develops from the zygote and grows within the mother plant to a certain size before growth is halted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F%D0%BD.jpg/330px-%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B7%D0%B8%D0%B5_%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%BC%D1%8F%D0%BD.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seed","wikidata":"Q40763"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Whole_Sunflower_seed.jpg/500px-Whole_Sunflower_seed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Whole_Sunflower_seed.jpg/1280px-Whole_Sunflower_seed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Whole_Sunflower_seed.jpg/500px-Whole_Sunflower_seed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Whole sunflower seeds","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhole_Sunflower_seed.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/1280px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or